## Question 1: Assign a Sequential Number to Each Order Per User

### Sample Table: Orders

| order_id | user_id | order_date | total_amount |
|---:|---:|---|---:|
| 101 | 1 | 2026-10-10 | 500.00 |
| 102 | 1 | 2026-10-15 | 700.00 |
| 103 | 1 | 2026-10-12 | 300.00 |
| 104 | 2 | 2026-10-08 | 450.00 |
| 105 | 2 | 2026-10-18 | 900.00 |
| 106 | 2 | 2026-10-11 | 600.00 |

### SQL Query

```sql
SELECT
    order_id,
    user_id,
    order_date,
    total_amount,
    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (
        PARTITION BY user_id
        ORDER BY order_date DESC, order_id DESC
    ) AS row_num
FROM Orders
ORDER BY user_id, row_num;
```

### Result Output

| order_id | user_id | order_date | total_amount | row_num |
|---:|---:|---|---:|---:|
| 102 | 1 | 2026-10-15 | 700.00 | 1 |
| 103 | 1 | 2026-10-12 | 300.00 | 2 |
| 101 | 1 | 2026-10-10 | 500.00 | 3 |
| 105 | 2 | 2026-10-18 | 900.00 | 1 |
| 106 | 2 | 2026-10-11 | 600.00 | 2 |
| 104 | 2 | 2026-10-08 | 450.00 | 3 |

**Explanation:** `ROW_NUMBER()` assigns a unique sequential number to each order within each user. `PARTITION BY user_id` restarts numbering for every user, while `ORDER BY order_date DESC` places the newest order first. `order_id DESC` breaks ties when dates are equal.

