## Question 2: Rank Songs by Streams Within Each Artist

### Sample Table: Songs

| song_id | artist | streams |
|---:|---|---:|
| 1 | Arijit Singh | 900000 |
| 2 | Arijit Singh | 750000 |
| 3 | Arijit Singh | 750000 |
| 4 | Taylor Swift | 1200000 |
| 5 | Taylor Swift | 950000 |
| 6 | Taylor Swift | 800000 |

### SQL Query

```sql
SELECT
    song_id,
    artist,
    streams,
    RANK() OVER (
        PARTITION BY artist
        ORDER BY streams DESC
    ) AS song_rank
FROM Songs
ORDER BY artist, song_rank, song_id;
```

### Result Output

| song_id | artist | streams | song_rank |
|---:|---|---:|---:|
| 1 | Arijit Singh | 900000 | 1 |
| 2 | Arijit Singh | 750000 | 2 |
| 3 | Arijit Singh | 750000 | 2 |
| 4 | Taylor Swift | 1200000 | 1 |
| 5 | Taylor Swift | 950000 | 2 |
| 6 | Taylor Swift | 800000 | 3 |

**Explanation:** `RANK()` assigns ranks according to stream counts within each artist. Songs with equal stream counts receive the same rank. The next rank is skipped after a tie, so the two Arijit Singh songs with 750,000 streams both receive rank 2.

